('MAT_C_03','Mathematics','C','K3','Probability','If you flip a fair coin 5 times and it lands on Heads every single time, what is the probability it lands on Heads on the 6th flip?','[{"id":"a","text":"Almost impossible, since Tails is \"due\".","points":1},{"id":"b","text":"100%, because it''s on a streak.","points":0},{"id":"c","text":"Exactly 50% (1/2), because each flip is an independent event.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"1/6","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Spatial Reasoning","Formula Dependency"]'::jsonb,1),
('MAT_C_04','Mathematics','C','K4','Inequalities','When solving the inequality -2x < 6, why do we flip the inequality sign to get x > -3?','[{"id":"a","text":"Because you always flip the sign in algebra.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"Because dividing or multiplying by a negative number reverses the order on the number line.","points":5},{"id":"c","text":"Because a negative answer requires a \"greater than\" sign.","points":1},{"id":"d","text":"You do not flip the sign; the answer is x < -3.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Graph Illiteracy","Spatial Reasoning"]'::jsonb,1),
('PHY_Z_01','Physics','Z','K1','Gravity & Falling Objects','You drop a heavy yam and a light pebble from the same height at the exact same time. What happens?','[{"id":"a","text":"The heavy yam hits the ground first because it has more weight.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"The light pebble hits the ground first because it faces less air resistance.","points":1},{"id":"c","text":"They both hit the ground at the exact same time.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"The yam falls faster at first, then the pebble catches up.","points":0}]'::jsonb,'Galileo proved that gravity accelerates all objects at the exact same rate (9.8 m/s²), regardless of their mass. Air resistance can slow lighter, flatter objects down (like a feather), but for dense objects like a yam and a pebble, the effect of air is negligible. Thus, they hit the ground simultaneously.',null,null,'["Reading Comprehension","Spatial Reasoning"]'::jsonb,3),
('PHY_Z_02','Physics','Z','K2','Force & Motion','A boy kicks a football high into the air. While the ball is flying in the air (and ignoring wind), what forces are pushing it FORWARD?','[{"id":"a","text":"The force of the kick stays with the ball and keeps pushing it.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"There is no forward force pushing it; it moves forward because of inertia (its starting motion).","points":5},{"id":"c","text":"Gravity pushes it forward in an arc.","points":0},{"id":"d","text":"The air pushes it forward from behind.","points":1}]'::jsonb,'This tests a classic misconception known as "impetus theory". Once the ball leaves the foot, the kick is over; there is no longer any forward force acting on it. It continues to move forward purely because of inertia (Newton''s First Law). The only force acting on it in flight is gravity pulling it downward.',null,null,'["Spatial Reasoning","Conceptual Application"]'::jsonb,3),
('PHY_Z_03','Physics','Z','K3','Heat Transfer','You are stirring a hot pot of egusi soup. Why does a metal spoon feel hotter in your hand than a wooden spoon?','[{"id":"a","text":"Metal creates its own heat when it touches the hot soup.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"Metal absorbs heat, but wood blocks heat completely.","points":1},{"id":"c","text":"Metal allows heat to travel through it quickly to your hand; wood does not.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"The metal spoon is heavier, so it attracts more heat.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Logical Deduction"]'::jsonb,3),
('PHY_Z_04','Physics','Z','K4','Energy Conservation','A generator at the market is running, powering a fridge. What is the main energy change happening?','[{"id":"a","text":"It creates electrical energy from nothing.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"It changes chemical energy in fuel into electrical energy, losing some as heat and sound.","points":5},{"id":"c","text":"It uses up all the fuel and destroys the energy to make cold air.","points":1},{"id":"d","text":"It turns sound energy into electrical energy.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Formula Dependency","Logical Deduction"]'::jsonb,3),
('PHY_F_01','Physics','F','K1','Newton''s Third Law','A fast-moving Danfo bus collides with a small stationary bicycle. During the crash, which experiences a greater impact force?','[{"id":"a","text":"The Danfo bus exerts a much larger force on the bicycle.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"The bicycle exerts a larger force on the Danfo bus.","points":0},{"id":"c","text":"They both exert the exact same amount of force on each other.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"The Danfo bus absorbs all the force since it is heavier.","points":1}]'::jsonb,'According to Newton''s Third Law, every action has an equal and opposite reaction. The force the bus exerts on the bicycle is exactly equal in magnitude to the force the bicycle exerts on the bus. The reason the bicycle is destroyed while the bus is barely dented is because the bicycle has much less mass, so the same force causes a much greater acceleration (damage) on it (F = ma).',null,null,'[]'::jsonb,null),
('PHY_F_02','Physics','F','K2','Electricity','In a basic circuit with a battery and a light bulb, what happens to the electric current after it passes through the bulb?','[{"id":"a","text":"The current is used up by the bulb to make light.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"The current is slightly less because the bulb consumed some of it.","points":1},{"id":"c","text":"The current remains exactly the same; it is not used up, only the energy is transferred.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"The current turns into heat and leaves the wire.","points":0}]'::jsonb,'Electric current is the flow of electrons, and electrons are never "used up" or destroyed in a circuit; they simply flow through it like water through a pipe. What gets used up is the electrical potential energy, which the bulb converts into light and heat energy.',null,null,'["Spatial Reasoning","Reading Comprehension"]'::jsonb,2),
('PHY_F_03','Physics','F','K3','Waves & Sound','When a radio is turned up louder, what physical property of the sound wave changes?','[{"id":"a","text":"The frequency of the wave increases.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"The speed of the sound wave increases.","points":1},{"id":"c","text":"The amplitude (height) of the wave increases.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"The wavelength of the wave becomes shorter.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Spatial Reasoning","Conceptual Application"]'::jsonb,1),
