('PHY_C_01','Physics','C','K1','Projectile Motion','A bullet is fired perfectly horizontally from a gun, and at the exact same instant, an identical bullet is dropped from the same height. Which hits the flat ground first?','[{"id":"a","text":"The dropped bullet hits first because it falls straight down.","points":1},{"id":"b","text":"The fired bullet hits first because it is traveling much faster.","points":0},{"id":"c","text":"They both hit the ground at the exact same time.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"It depends on the mass of the bullet.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Graph Illiteracy","Conceptual Application"]'::jsonb,2),
('PHY_C_02','Physics','C','K2','Circular Motion','A stone is tied to a string and swung in a horizontal circle. If the string suddenly snaps, what path will the stone take?','[{"id":"a","text":"It will fly straight outward, away from the center.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"It will continue in a curved path for a short time before straightening out.","points":1},{"id":"c","text":"It will fly off in a straight line tangent to the circle at the point it broke.","points":5},{"id":"d","text":"It will spiral outward gradually.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Spatial Reasoning","Conceptual Application"]'::jsonb,3),
('PHY_C_03','Physics','C','K3','Electromagnetic Induction','When a solid copper ring is dropped through a strong magnetic field, it falls slower than if dropped outside the field. Why?','[{"id":"a","text":"The magnetic field physically pushes on the copper atoms to slow them down.","points":1},{"id":"b","text":"The falling ring induces a current, which creates an opposing magnetic field that repels the fall (Lenz''s Law).","points":5},{"id":"c","text":"Copper is naturally magnetic and is attracted to the magnet.","points":0},{"id":"d","text":"The air resistance increases significantly inside a magnetic field.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Logical Deduction","Conceptual Application"]'::jsonb,1),
('PHY_C_04','Physics','C','K4','Quantum/Photoelectric','In the photoelectric effect, increasing the INTENSITY (brightness) of the incoming light will...','[{"id":"a","text":"Increase the kinetic energy (speed) of the emitted electrons.","points":0},{"id":"b","text":"Increase the number of electrons emitted per second, but not their maximum speed.","points":5},{"id":"c","text":"Decrease the threshold frequency required.","points":1},{"id":"d","text":"Have absolutely no effect on the electrons.","points":0}]'::jsonb,null,null,null,'["Formula Dependency","Graph Illiteracy"]'::jsonb,1),
